\begin{abstract}
This publication outlines the complete design package for the Prism-Aetheris Substrate Encryption Engine (PASSEE), a high-density computational substrate mapping discrete data streams into continuous wave-packet amplitudes. By simulating optical refraction, gravitational lensing, and chromatic dispersion through non-Euclidean computational manifolds, PASSEE bypasses classical algebraic cryptographic limitations. Structural analysis and empirical testing verify a Grade A+ resilience rating up to a 44.8\,$\mu$J structural threshold with zero data drop rate and complete Hamiltonian invariance ($\Delta H / H < 1.2 \times 10^{-14}$). To transition this simulation paradigm to high-performance edge photonics, we detail the complete physical layer implementation specifications—designated as the Refractive Photonic Encryption Core (RPEC-1)—featuring a 64-channel 112\,Gbps PAM4 SerDes interface, a cascaded Mach-Zehnder Interferometer (MZI) mesh, and an automated 50\,$\mu$s polarization drift tracking loop.
\end{abstract}

